.TH HOMELAB-BACKUP-CLIENT 8 "2026-08-29" "homelab-backup" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
homelab-backup-client \- borg-based backup/restore client for homelab hosts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B homelab-backup-client
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B homelab-backup-client
pushes encrypted
.BR borg (1)
archives from this host to a centralized backup server over SSH, driven
by
.IR /etc/homelab/backup/client/config.yml .
It exists to let an administrator restore a homelab host's config or
data to a point in time after a disaster; it is not limited to
.I /etc/homelab/*
configs specifically (see
.B mode:
in config.yml) but is not a general end-user-data (Drive files,
mailboxes) backup tool either.
.PP
Enrollment with the backup server is handled through homelab-api/
homelab-cli: this host's SSH public key is submitted once
.RB ( setup ),
and applied automatically by
.BR homelab-backup-server 's
own periodic reconciliation job \- no manual admin command is required
on the server. Each backup/check run's outcome is also optionally
reported back through the same control plane for later visibility
.RB ( "homelab-cli backup host-runs" ).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR setup " (alias: " configure )
Interactively (re-)configure this host: config values, an
auto-generated per-host encryption passphrase, backup mode, schedule,
and homelab-api enrollment. Run this after installing the package, and
safe to re-run any time afterward to change settings (existing values
are left alone unless you choose to change them). Not available outside
of a Debian package install (see
.B install/install.sh
in a git checkout instead).
.TP
.BI "backup " "[--dry-run] [--once] [--config PATH]"
Run a backup per config.yml. This is also what the installed systemd
unit/timer invokes; running it with no subcommand at all is still
accepted for compatibility and behaves identically.
.B --dry-run
prints the resolved sources, exclude patterns, and the exact
.B borg create
/
.B borg prune
commands without executing them.
.B --once
forces a single run even when
.B schedule.mode
is
.BR continuous .
.TP
.BI "check " "[--config PATH]"
Run
.B borg check
(read-only integrity verification, never
.BR --repair ,
which is a deliberate manual admin action) against this host's own
repo. Also what the
.B homelab-backup-client-check
timer invokes, on the schedule set by
.B integrity_check.mode
in config.yml (default
.BR monthly ).
.TP
.BI "list-archives " "[--host ID] [--server S] [--ssh-user U] [--location DIR] [--ssh-key PATH] [--passphrase-file PATH] [--archive NAME] [--names-only]"
List archives in a repo, or (with
.BR --archive )
one specific archive's file contents instead. All override options
default to the local config.yml (with the backup server itself
auto-discovered via homelab-cli if
.B backup_server
is blank); override them to inspect a different host's repo, e.g.
during disaster recovery with the admin recovery key (see
.B setup-admin-recovery-key.sh ).
.B --names-only
prints just archive names, one per line, no other detail \- for
scripting, and what shell completion uses to offer real archive names
after
.B --archive
on both this command and
.BR restore .
.TP
.BI "restore --archive NAME --target DIR " "[--paths ...] [--dry-run] [same overrides as list-archives]"
Extract an archive into
.IR DIR ,
which is created if missing. Extraction always goes into an explicit
target directory, never directly over
.IR / ,
so a restore is reviewable before anything is copied into place by
hand.
.B --dry-run
lists what would be extracted without writing anything.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/homelab/backup/client/config.yml
Per-host configuration. Mode 600, root:root \- contains the repo
encryption passphrase. See the shipped
.I config.example.yml
for the full documented schema.
.TP
.I /etc/homelab/backup/client/ssh/id_ed25519
This host's dedicated SSH key, restricted server-side to
.B "borg serve --restrict-to-repository"
for this host's own repo path only.
.TP
.I /etc/homelab/backup/client/key-escrow/
Exported borg key material (a
.B "borg key export"
and a
.B --paper
copy), written on first backup. Not replicated off this host
automatically \- copy both this and the passphrase above to a secrets
manager and an offline/paper copy.
.TP
.I /etc/homelab/backup/client/homelab-cli/
This host's own isolated homelab-cli session directory (JWT/refresh
token), separate from any interactively-logged-in human's
.IR ~/.config/homelab-cli .
.SH CONTROL PLANE (homelab-api / homelab-cli)
If
.B homelab_cli.disabled
is not set in config.yml (the default), this host maintains its own
homelab-cli session and uses it for three things: submitting its pubkey
for enrollment during
.BR setup ,
discovering
.BR backup_server / ssh_backup_server_username / backup_server_location
when left blank in config.yml, and reporting each
.B backup
/
.B check
run's outcome
.RB ( "homelab-cli backup report-run" / report-check ).
Run-history reporting is best-effort \- a control-plane hiccup only logs
a warning, and never fails or blocks an actual backup. Set
.B homelab_cli.disabled: true
to opt a host out of the control plane entirely (requires
.B backup_server
to be set explicitly).
.SH DISASTER RECOVERY
See the
.B "Restoring onto a fresh/replacement host"
section of this package's README for the full runbook: generate an
admin recovery SSH key once on the backup server
.RI ( setup-admin-recovery-key.sh ),
retrieve the escrowed passphrase for the lost host, then
.B list-archives
/
.B restore
against its repo using
.B --host
and
.B --ssh-key
to point at the recovery key instead of the (destroyed) per-host key.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR borg (1),
.BR homelab-cli (1)
